@id("close-workorder-main-with-human-approval")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"MaintData___close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Halvern___get_approval"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    output.decision: Drupe::Halvern___get_approval_Output_decision::"GRANTED",
    output.approver_kind: Drupe::Halvern___get_approval_Output_approver_kind::"HUMAN"
  }
};

// Read-only lookups. These must be permitted so the approval lookup
// is recorded as a response and can serve as evidence for the close.
@id("allow-read-only-lookups")
permit (
  principal,
  action in [
    Drupe::Action::"Halvern___get_approval",
    Drupe::Action::"Dorvane___get_approval",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_failure_codes",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_my_assigned_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_schedule_calendar",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder_costs",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder_kpis",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___get_workorder_tasks",
    Drupe::Action::"MaintData___list_workorders"
  ],
  resource
);
```

I used `Halvern___get_approval` as the only approval evidence, because its output has fixed values (`GRANTED` and `HUMAN`) that the policy can check exactly. `Dorvane___get_approval` returns free-form strings, so I can't tell what a human approval looks like in its output. It is allowed as a read-only lookup but does not count towards a close.

Nothing permits `approve_workorder`, `assign_technician`, `cancel_workorder`, `update_workorder` or `generate_work_order`, so they are denied by default. The agent therefore cannot approve work orders itself.

The `GRANTED` and `HUMAN` checks are written as enum entity references (e.g. `Drupe::Halvern___get_approval_Output_decision::"GRANTED"`), because the schema declares those output fields as enum entities rather than strings. The reference's own examples only show plain string literals, so I haven't confirmed the engine accepts this form in an output pattern.

